-name">God's Wife of Amun</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">God's Wife of Amun was the title of the highest-ranking priestess of the god Amun at Thebes.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/godavaya"><span class="enc-name">Godavaya</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Godavaya is an archaeological site on the south coast of Sri Lanka, near the mouth of the Walawe river in Hambantota District.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/godefroy-maurice-de-la-tour-dauvergne"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/godefroy-maurice-de-la-tour-dauvergne.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Godefroy Maurice de La Tour d'Auvergne</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Godefroy Maurice de La Tour d'Auvergne was a French nobleman, Duke of Bouillon and a nephew of Marshal Turenne.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/godegisel"><span class="enc-name">Godegisel</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Godegisel (died 406) was king of the Hasding Vandals at the time of the great crossing of the Rhine.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-binaisa"><span class="enc-name">Godfrey Binaisa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Godfrey Binaisa was a Ugandan lawyer who served as attorney general under Milton Obote in the 1960s and as president of Uganda from June 1979 to May 1980, after the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-huggins"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/godfrey-huggins.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Godfrey Huggins</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Godfrey Huggins was an English-born surgeon who became prime minister of Southern Rhodesia from 1933 to 1953, the longest tenure in the Commonwealth.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-i-of-louvain"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/godfrey-i-of-louvain.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Godfrey I of Louvain</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Godfrey I of Louvain, called the Bearded, was Count of Louvain and Brussels and, from 1106, Duke of Lower Lorraine.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-iii-count-of-louvain"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/godfrey-iii-count-of-louvain.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Godfrey III, Count of Louvain</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Godfrey III was count of Louvain and Brussels and titular duke of Lower Lorraine from 1142 until his death in 1190.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-iii-duke-of-lower-lorraine"><span class="enc-name">Godfrey III, Duke of Lower Lorraine</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Godfrey III, called the Bearded, was the son of Gothelo I and became duke of Upper Lorraine in 1044.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/godfrey-of-bouillon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Godfrey of Bouillon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Godfrey of Bouillon was Duke of Lower Lorraine and a leader of the First Crusade, son of Count Eustace II of Boulogne.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/godin-tepe"><span class="enc-name">Godin Tepe</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Godin Tepe is an archaeological site in the Kangavar valley of western Iran, on the route through the Zagros Mountains toward